  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions anticipated through early \nportions of the week.\n\nIn the wake of some morning shower activity across northeastern\nMaryland, any residual clouds have largely cleared out. The air\nmass remains seasonably dry with dew points holding steady in\nthe mid 50s to low 60s.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to slowly build toward the area\nthrough Tuesday, resulting in dry conditions and lower humidity\nlevels. High temperatures will generally be in the upper 70s to\nmid 80s, with overnight lows in the 50s to lower 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warmer and more humid conditions return mid-\nweek, with thunderstorm chances returning by Thursday.\n\nHeading into Wednesday, dry, pleasant conditions continue into \nmuch of the day and afternoon. As an upper level ridge over the \nmidwest begins to flatten in amplitude, southwesterly mid-level \nwinds advect tropical moisture northward into our region. As \nthis tropical air mass moves through our region, humid \nconditions return through the end of the week. A weak piece of\nshortwave energy is also expected to approach the region\nWednesday evening, which could spark a few storms in the\nevening/overnight hours. \n\nAs the upper trough over the northeast CONUS starts to push more\noffshore on Wednesday, we will see some subtle rises in 500 hPa\nheights. This, paired with a developing southerly surface wind\npattern will yield more typical August warmth and increased\nhumidity. Expect high temperatures in the upper 80s to near 90,\nalong with dew points increasing into the low to mid 60s. The \nridge doesn't will only be overhead during the day though, as \nanother piece of shortwave energy rotates through late in the \nevening into the overnight. There won't be a lot of instability\nleft over when this rotates through, but there may be enough to \npop off a few overnight showers. \n\nBy Thursday, after a day of southerly flow ahead of an\napproaching cold front from the west, humidity will jump\nmarkedly. Temperatures remain in the mid 80s to low 90s, but dew\npoints jump into the upper 60s and perhaps even into the low\n70s. The aforementioned cold front will come through during the\nday Thursday. If timed during peak heating, there could be\naround 1000 to 1500 J/kg of MLCAPE to work with. The shortwave\nis subtle, with only about 20 kts of deep layer shear showing up\nin most guidance. So, the threat for organized severe weather is\nrather low. Additionally, this is not one of the extreme\nheat/humidity setups, so thinking is a more isolated wind threat\nat this point. Latest AI medium-range guidance generally \nagrees, painting anywhere from 5 to 15 percent severe probs over\nthe region depending on which one you use. \n\nBy Friday, the front should be mostly offshore. However, there\nis still the slight chance that it lingers somewhere near/east\nof I-95, which could bring renewed afternoon storm chances.",
